**Read:** [Microbiology — Parker and colleagues, OpenStax](../sources/Microbiology-OpenStax.lingua#r_4K6CQQBY6AWTZCD0EJ5PRFGFTW), chapter 9. Revisit the shared exponential and logarithmic quantities.

An original ideal population model assumes every member doubles at the same fixed interval g, without death, resource limitation or loss. It gives N(t)=N₀2^(t/g). Starting with 100 and using a hypothetical g=20 minutes gives 6400 after two hours. The arithmetic illustrates time dependence; it does not predict a real organism in an unspecified environment.

Transport can introduce or remove organisms while biological processes change the count. Surface attachment and community structure can further change observation and response. A result at receipt may not characterize the same material after storage.

**Work:** Derive the ideal result by repeated doubling and by an exponential expression. Add a finite resource bound conceptually and identify where the unlimited model fails. For a published process, list the environmental and sampling evidence needed to interpret a change over time; no culturing experiment is required for this exercise.

**Return:** [Clean manufacturing and contamination control](../Clean-manufacturing-and-contamination-control.lingua#r_7D8SQ8BVQDZQBDBGC6DP0JAMZN).
**Continue in clean manufacturing:** [Cleaning, disinfection, sterilization and preservation](Cleaning-disinfection-sterilization-and-preservation.lingua#r_0CKR7RH81CKM2Y33N9X5FAXYB7).

